It is important to keep in mind that the tax code is a living document. It changes from year to year, sometimes even week to week. And the IRS has the authority to interpret the code in any manner it sees fit. One of the most significant changes in the tax code over the last several years has come with the proliferation of tax shelters and tax avoidance vehicles that have been developed to circumvent the law. As these shelters have become more prevalent, the IRS has felt compelled to more closely scrutinize tax returns in order to ensure that taxpayers aren't taking advantage of these opportunities to reduce their tax burden. Additionally, as the tax code has become more complicated, the IRS has had to increase its efforts to ensure that taxpayers comply with the law, which can result a higher audit rate.
Taxpayers who have not been auditors should also be aware that the IRS has the authority to impose penalties for a number different reasons, including but not limited to:
Failure to file a tax return;
Failure of a partnership to file a tax return;
Failure of an individual or a partnership, estate, or trust to file a return by the due date;
Making a fraudulent return or claim for refund;
Making a false return or claim for refund;
Making a return or claim for refund that is willfully false, or is made under circumstances where the taxpayer knows that it is substantially incorrect;
Making a return or claim for refund that is knowingly false, or is made under a reckless disregard of whether it is substantially correct or not;
